**It pauses the current async function’s continuation.** It does not block the JavaScript thread while waiting for the promise to settle.

```js
async function load() {
  console.log('A');
  await Promise.resolve();
  console.log('B');
}
load();
console.log('C');
```

The output is **A, C, B**. The function starts immediately, reaches await, and hands control back. Its remaining work resumes as a microtask after the current synchronous work finishes.
